"""The painter's pixels, on this machine's own GPU.

    tests/painter-pixels [words ...]
    tests/painter-pixels --capture pi.local:1 [NAME=VALUE ...]

The painter is built for this machine (tests/gpupainter.py) and run with
no screen; what it composed is read back with `picture glass` and the
glass stream, and held to what it should be:

    twice          one scene composed twice is the same pixels, exactly
    the stream     the I420 stream is the glass in BT.709 at studio range
    streams        several at once, each the glass at its own size, and
                   the overlay only in the one that asks for it
    align          a frame against the top, the middle or the bottom
    blending       QOI, PNG and JPEG frames over cells, alpha and all
    resized        the glass resized 200 times under a base, a layer
                   and two streams, each picture right, nothing growing
    against v1     cells and the characters drawn as geometry, against
                   v1's `picture glass` of the same scene on a Pi

The last needs the reference in tests/pixels, which `--capture
HOST:WALL` takes from a v1 painter: it attaches to the wall's base, shows
the scene, takes a picture of the glass, and detaches. Ask the wall's
`sessions` first: attaching displaces whoever holds the base. NAME=VALUE
is the painter's environment there that changes what it draws, kept with
the reference and given to the painter here: the Pis' painters run with
BITWALL_EVEN_CELLS=1, which rounds a cell to an even width and a height
of a multiple of four. The reference is the whole glass, neither cut nor
scaled, so the scene sits in its top rows and the rest compresses to
nothing.

Where no GPU painter builds (anything but a Mac, or Windows with
BITWALL_PAINT_EXE naming one), every check is skipped and says so.
"""
